  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 322 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nGuidance remains tightly clustered through the early half of the \nweek with only a slight increase in spread by Wednesday. There is \nhigh confidence in mild conditions through at least Tuesday with \nwidespread 60s for most areas, and low-70s across SE Missouri and SW \nIllinois. Mild conditions continue into Wednesday for much of\nthe area, though an approaching cold front and arrival of a\nsurface low will determine how quickly cooler air arrives\nthrough the later half of the day.\n\nA surface low begins to develop over Kansas early Wednesday morning \nas an upper level shortwave moves southeast out of the northern \nPlains. The surface low tracks eastward through Missouri and \nIllinois, eventually deepening as the northern and southern jets \nbegin to phase over the eastern U.S. around Thursday. This \nevolution becomes a key player later in rain potential and cold\nair advection. First, however, a more vigorous, amplifying \nupper level trough digs southward out of Canada, eventually \nconsolidating with the shortwave from the northern Plains. \nConsequently, this is where guidance begins to diverge with \ntiming and placement of these features. \n\nDeterministic GFS guidance is notably more bullish late \nWednesday with deep saturation along and east of the Mississippi\nRiver while the ECMWF phases later and strengthens the surface \nlow slightly further east. The difference between the two is \nlighter rain showers in the ECMWF solution with GFS showing \nbetter potential for a light rain, but enough to soak the\nsurface. That being said, NBM probabilistic data only shows \n40-50% probabilities for measurable rainfall across the \nsoutheastern CWA, while many of the wetter GFS ensemble members\nland at or below 0.40 of an inch. The lower quartiles across\nmajority ensembles range from zero to a few hundredths of an \ninch across southeastern sections of the CWA. Therefore, while \nrain chances have crept up, there remains a considerable amount \nof uncertainty in rainfall amounts with higher confidence that\nthe northwestern sections of the CWA miss out on measurable \nrainfall.\n\nBy Thursday, much colder air arrives behind the surface low as \nit departs to the east. Rainfall potential looks to be low \nthrough the remainder of the period. However, confidence is \nincreasing that the region remains under the western edge of the \nlongwave upper trough. The cyclonic side of these systems can often \nlead to diurnal clouds that sometimes produce drizzle or a few \nsprinkles. This is rarely picked up by lower spatial and temporal \nresolution of long range guidance with better insight as it is \ncaptured by medium and short range models. The general theme will be \na trend toward below normal temperatures with the fall chill \nextending into next weekend. Some ensemble members include highs in \nthe 40s over parts of the region Thursday and Friday. Temperatures \neventually moderate at the tail end of the period, though ensemble \nspread increases and provides less confidence on the timing and \nmagnitude of warmth.",
